{
  "content": "<updated_cheatsheet>\n- **Rebuild the task spec from the latest user turn, then merge earlier user turns as still-valid constraints.** Treat later turns as refinements or narrowing updates, not new independent tasks. Re-check every prior assumption against the newest wording so outdated broader interpretations do not survive.\n\n- **Separate user-stated constraints from assistant-invented inferences.** Only promote a condition into the task spec if the user explicitly said it or the dialogue/schema directly supports it. If support is missing, mark the point as unsupported or uncertain rather than silently baking it into the requirement.\n\n- **Compress incremental disclosure into the smallest complete final requirement.** When the user narrows the target over time, prefer the most specific surviving intent and discard broader earlier interpretations. The final spec should reflect the user\u2019s current objective, not an accumulated superset of all prior variants.\n\n- **Check alignment at the output-shape level, not just at the logic/filter level.** A response can be structurally or semantically related yet still be wrong if it returns the wrong entity, granularity, or projection. Verify separately: what is being returned, what is being filtered, and what aggregation level the user asked for.\n\n- **Flag missing required outputs as concrete gaps.** If the user asks for multiple attributes, entities, or dimensions, a response that omits one of them is incomplete even if the rest is correct. Extra correctness in one area does not compensate for an absent required field, join, or result dimension.\n\n- **Treat \u201cextra\u201d requested content as overreach once the user narrows the ask.** When the latest turn says \u201cjust X,\u201d \u201conly X,\u201d or otherwise restricts scope, additional columns, summaries, or adjacent details should be marked as divergence even if they would be harmless or technically valid.\n\n- **Distinguish row-level filtering from column-level projection.** The analyzer should independently verify whether the assistant got the right subset of records and whether it returned the right information about those records. A correct filter does not justify the wrong output shape, and vice versa.\n\n- **When the user asks for absence/none/zero-match conditions, look for negative evidence patterns and inversion errors.** Responses that enumerate matches or positive cases can be the opposite of what was requested. Preserve the current negative condition and discard prior \u201cfind the matching items\u201d framing that would anchor analysis in the wrong direction.\n\n- **Validate final-query completeness, not just intermediate progress.** If the assistant has only discussed parts of the reasoning, note whether it actually synthesized all surviving constraints into one answer. Intermediate narrowing is useful only if it still supports the final deliverable.\n\n- **Use schema-grounded validation over semantic guessing.** If the available fields do not directly support a concept, any derived proxy should be treated as uncertain and not counted as confirmed alignment. Prefer explicit evidence from the conversation/schema over plausible but unsupported interpretation.\n\n- **Distinguish appropriate caution from spec drift.** Clarifying questions are useful while the task is still underspecified, but once enough information exists, the analyzer should resolve the requirement from the available user turns instead of preserving an unresolved branch.\n\n- **In edit/correction modes, remove misleading exploratory reasoning while keeping validated constraints.** The goal is not to preserve the whole earlier thought process; it is to retain only the joins, filters, and targets that still match the user\u2019s final intent and discard speculative paths that would anchor future reasoning incorrectly.\n\n- **When multiple assistant turns are mostly right, focus on the remaining mismatch.** Do not relitigate earlier acceptable reasoning. Identify the highest-leverage divergence, often an output-schema or granularity error, especially when the assistant is close but not exact.\n\n- **Prefer minimal-valid solutions when the user\u2019s request is narrow.** If the final user wording is concise, the analyzer should favor the smallest answer that satisfies it and treat added adjacent information as likely drift unless the user explicitly asked for it.\n\n- **Track the current entity being returned versus related records used only for filtering.** A response may correctly use related data to determine eligibility but still fail if it returns the wrong object type or wrong level of aggregation. The analyzer should explicitly verify the final deliverable matches the user\u2019s stated target.\n\n- **Keep the task spec outcome-oriented and tied to the user\u2019s final wording.** Build the spec around what the user wants revealed, counted, or checked, then use that spec to test every assistant turn for missing, added, or broadened outputs. Avoid preserving older broadened interpretations once the user has tightened the goal.\n\n- **Mark partially aligned answers as risky when they are incomplete.** Near-miss responses are especially prone to further drift if not corrected. The analyzer should explicitly surface the remaining mismatch rather than treating partial success as full success.\n</updated_cheatsheet>",
